{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "milestone": "M60",
  "rule": "Objective-C relationship LINE truth: in range, and does the line contain the target",
  "generatedAt": "2026-09-30T03:37:14.421Z",
  "method": "every relationship in the router's universe (329 files, 24114 edges). For each edge: is the line within the file, and does the SOURCE TEXT of that line contain the edge's target — under a per-type rule, because \"the line mentions the target\" is the right question for a member declaration and the wrong one for a superclass member that is not in the file at all. The arbiter is the file's own text, which the analyzer has no access to and which the F5 oracle cannot supply either, since it derives truth from the same tree.",
  "why": "presence is the easy half and it is the half that survives being wrong: a row pointing at line 1 of a 600-line file is present and useless, and a wrong line is worse than a missing one because a consumer lands somewhere confident",
  "files": 329,
  "totalEdges": 24114,
  "rules": {
    "contains": {
      "rule": "exact",
      "why": "a member is declared on the member's own declaration; the OBJCD7 defect was exactly this relationship publishing the CONTAINER's line for member rows. Selector-shaped members are matched by leaf over a small window, because an Objective-C selector WRAPS and no single line carries the full string"
    },
    "extends": {
      "rule": "exact",
      "why": "the superclass name is written on the @interface/@protocol line that the edge is about"
    },
    "implements": {
      "rule": "exact",
      "why": "a conformed protocol is named in the @interface's protocol list, on that line"
    },
    "uses": {
      "rule": "plausible",
      "why": "a value read's target is a name in scope, which need not be on the read's own line (a parameter is read far from its declaration) — so presence on the line is necessary, not sufficient"
    },
    "calls": {
      "rule": "plausible",
      "why": "a selector is written at the send, and the edge's target is the FULL selector, so the LEAF is what must appear — over a window, since a send routinely wraps across lines in this language"
    },
    "instantiates": {
      "rule": "plausible",
      "why": "the class receiver is on the line for [[Foo alloc] init] and [Foo new], but a nested alloc puts the receiver one line up"
    },
    "overrides": {
      "rule": "not-decidable",
      "why": "the target is a SUPERCLASS member, absent from the subclass file by definition — an override's line is the overriding declaration, which is correct"
    },
    "references": {
      "rule": "not-decidable",
      "why": "the target is a module name from an #import, and whether the line is the import or the use is a design choice this lane has not pinned"
    }
  },
